<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Indies</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The West Indies are the islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> and the Greater Antilles to the Lesser Antilles, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s belief that he had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. From the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> colonies there worked by enslaved Africans and trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The British islands formed the short-lived West Indies Federation from 1958 to 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-indies.jpg" alt="West Indies" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heraldry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Indies_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The West Indies form an island subregion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, surrounded by the North Atlantic Ocean and the Caribbean Sea. They comprise 13 independent island countries and 18 dependencies in three archipelagos: the Greater Antilles, the Lesser Antilles and the Lucayan Archipelago, which includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> and the Turks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caicos-islands">Caicos Islands</a>. The term is often used interchangeably with Caribbean. The islands are a geologically complex system of some 7,000 islands and islets stretching over 3,000 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> peninsula to the northern coast of Venezuela.</p><p>The Greater Antilles are the oldest group and hold the largest islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>. The Lesser Antilles are a volcanic island arc with 19 active volcanoes, and the Lucayan islands are low islands on the Bahama Platform.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Many cultures were indigenous to these islands, with evidence dating some of them to the mid-sixth millennium BCE. Christopher Columbus, who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1492 seeking a western sea route to the spice trade of the Indies, believed he had reached the easternmost part of them. The Spanish therefore called the new lands Indias Occidentales, the West Indies, to tell them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, and the term first applied to all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Later the Spanish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, French, British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a> were distinguished by the European power to which they belonged.</p><p>In the late sixteenth century French, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants and privateers began to attack Spanish and Portuguese shipping in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. They gained a foothold in the Lesser Antilles with the colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> in 1624 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> in 1626. When the Sugar Revolution took off in the mid-seventeenth century they brought in thousands of enslaved Africans to work the fields and mills, who replaced or joined the indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> and the European indentured servants. In 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> sold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for US$25 million in gold, creating the United States Virgin Islands.</p><p>Between 1958 and 1962 the United Kingdom reorganised its island territories, except the British Virgin Islands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, into the West Indies Federation, in the hope that it would become a single independent nation. It had limited powers, practical problems and little popular support. Nine provinces later became independent states and four became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories">British Overseas Territories</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Indies” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Danish trading companies dealt in Asian tea, textiles and sugar from the <b>West Indies</b>, much of it re-exported.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Saint-Domingue became the richest colony of the French <b>West Indies</b>, the Pearl of the Antilles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The people now called the Taíno lived across most of the <b>West Indies</b> when Europeans arrived, from the Lucayan Archipelago through Cuba, Jamaica, Hispaniola and Puerto Rico to Guadeloupe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In London, the imperial capital, students, lawyers and clergy from West Africa and the <b>West Indies</b> met one another and visiting Black Americans and came to see their struggles as one.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Federations created by colonial powers, such as the <b>West Indies</b> Federation and the Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland, failed within a few years, and in the 19th century Gran Colombia and the Federal Republic of Central America had dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1778 French ships carried some 13,000 captives a year to the French <b>West Indies</b>, and Saint-Domingue alone held 406,000–465,000 enslaved people around 1789; there deaths outran births, as five to ten in every hundred died each year.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
